    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The height of $H$ above the square base $ABCD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3.7\\text{ cm}$",
          "worked": "$O$ is the midpoint of the base diagonal $AC$. In the base and then in right triangle $AOH$:\n$$\\begin{align*}AC^2&=10^2+10^2\\\\AC&=10\\sqrt2\\text{ cm}\\\\AO&=\\frac{10\\sqrt2}{2}=5\\sqrt2\\text{ cm}\\\\OH^2&=AH^2-AO^2\\\\OH^2&=8^2-(5\\sqrt2)^2=14\\\\OH&=\\sqrt{14}\\text{ cm}\\approx3.7\\text{ cm}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\angle OAH$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$27.9^\\circ$",
          "worked": "In right triangle $AOH$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\angle OAH&=\\frac{AO}{AH}\\\\\\cos\\angle OAH&=\\frac{5\\sqrt2}{8}\\\\\\angle OAH&=\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt2}{8}\\right)\\approx27.9^\\circ\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Find $\\angle OMH$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$36.8^\\circ$",
          "worked": "$OM=5\\text{ cm}$ because $O$ is the centre of the square and $M$ is the midpoint of a side. In right triangle $MOH$:\n$$\\begin{align*}OM&=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\text{ cm}\\\\\\tan\\angle OMH&=\\frac{OH}{OM}\\\\\\tan\\angle OMH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}\\\\\\angle OMH&=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}\\right)\\approx36.8^\\circ\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Let $P$ be a point on $AH$ such that $\\angle APB=\\angle APD=90^\\circ$. Find $\\angle BPD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p12-q6-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "129.9^\\circ",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p12-q6-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Find the perpendicular lengths ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "PB=PD",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then use the cosine rule in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\triangle BPD",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The equal slant edges give $BH=DH=AH=8\\text{ cm}$. Use the cosine rule in $ABH$ to find the angle shared by $ABH$ and right triangle $ABP$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\angle BAH&=\\frac{AB^2+AH^2-BH^2}{2(AB)(AH)}\\\\\\cos\\angle BAH&=\\frac{10^2+8^2-8^2}{2(10)(8)}=\\frac58\\\\AP&=AB\\cos\\angle BAH\\\\AP&=10\\times\\frac58=\\frac{25}{4}\\text{ cm}\\end{align*}$$\nRight triangles $ABP$ and $ADP$ have equal hypotenuses and share $AP$, so $PB=PD$. By Pythagoras:\n$$\\begin{align*}PB^2&=AB^2-AP^2\\\\PB^2&=10^2-\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)^2=\\frac{975}{16}\\\\PB&=PD=\\frac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\text{ cm}\\\\BD&=10\\sqrt2\\text{ cm}\\end{align*}$$\nUse the cosine rule in $BPD$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\angle BPD&=\\frac{PB^2+PD^2-BD^2}{2(PB)(PD)}\\\\\\cos\\angle BPD&=\\frac{\\frac{975}{16}+\\frac{975}{16}-(10\\sqrt2)^2}{2\\times\\frac{975}{16}}=-\\frac{25}{39}\\\\\\angle BPD&=\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(-\\frac{25}{39}\\right)\\approx129.9^\\circ\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 142
      }
    ]
